<h1>HTTP &amp; TLS</h1>
<p>Games talk to the outside world: fetching a news panel, posting a high score, connecting to a service over an encrypted channel. <strong>HTTP</strong> is the standard request/response protocol for those calls. <strong>TLS</strong> is the encryption layer that makes them safe on public networks. DemiEngine provides both as Lua services so gameplay code can call a web API without stopping the game, and can encrypt peer connections when the session requires it. This page explains those APIs from the ground up.</p>

<h2>Why asynchronous HTTP</h2>
<p>A web request can take hundreds of milliseconds — an eternity in a frame budget. The HTTP service is therefore <strong>asynchronous</strong>: you start a request, the game keeps running, and you poll for completion. That design keeps the frame loop responsive and makes timeouts explicit instead of hanging the game.</p>

<h2>Starting a request</h2>
<p>Import the HTTP service and start a request with <code>get</code>, <code>post</code>, or the general <code>request</code> form:</p>
<pre><code>local Http = require("demi.network.http")

local req = Http.get("https://demiengine.de/api/news")</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>require("demi.network.http")</code> — imports the HTTP service table.</li>
  <li><code>Http.get(url)</code> — starts a GET request. Returns the request handle, or <code>nil</code>, <code>err</code> when the request could not even be started (bad URL, unsupported scheme, over the size caps).</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>local req = Http.post("https://demiengine.de/api/scores",
    { json = { name = "Skyforge", score = 1200 } })</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Http.post(url, options)</code> — starts a POST request. <code>options.json</code> is a Lua table serialized as a JSON body (use <code>body</code> for a raw string instead — the two are exclusive).</li>
</ul>
<p>The general form accepts every option at once:</p>
<pre><code>local req, err = Http.request({
    url = "https://demiengine.de/api/news",
    method = "GET",
    headers = { ["accept"] = "application/json" },
    json = nil,                  -- or body = "raw text"; never both
    timeout_ms = 30000,
    connect_timeout_ms = 5000,
    max_response_bytes = 16 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_header_bytes = 64 * 1024,
    ca_certificate_pem = trusted_ca_pem_text
})</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>url</code></td><td>Full request URL, <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>method</code></td><td>HTTP method string such as <code>GET</code> or <code>POST</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>headers</code></td><td>Table of request header names and string values.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json</code></td><td>Lua table serialized as a JSON body. Mutually exclusive with <code>body</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>body</code></td><td>Raw string body. Mutually exclusive with <code>json</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>timeout_ms</code></td><td>Whole-request timeout in milliseconds. Default <code>30000</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>connect_timeout_ms</code></td><td>Connection setup timeout in milliseconds. Default <code>5000</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>max_response_bytes</code></td><td>Cap on response size. Default 16 MiB; larger responses fail instead of exhausting memory.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>max_header_bytes</code></td><td>Cap on response header size. Default 64 KiB.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ca_certificate_pem</code></td><td>Optional explicit PEM trust bundle for this request. A nonempty bundle replaces the default trust sources; it is not appended to them. Certificate and hostname verification stay enabled.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><code>Http.request(...)</code> returns <code>request</code>, <code>nil</code> on success (a handle and no error), or <code>nil</code>, <code>err</code> when the request cannot start. A started request is not a finished one — check it every frame.</p>

<h2>Waiting for the response</h2>
<pre><code>function Game:on_update(dt)
    if not req then return end
    if not req:done() then return end
    local res = req:response()
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>req:done()</code> — returns a boolean: <code>true</code> when the request finished (successfully or not), <code>false</code> while it is in flight.</li>
  <li><code>req:response()</code> — returns the response table once <code>done()</code> is <code>true</code>, or <code>nil</code> if no response is available.</li>
  <li><code>req:cancel()</code> — requests cancellation. Returns nothing; <code>done()</code> becomes <code>true</code> with a cancellation error.</li>
</ul>
<p>The response table:</p>
<pre><code>local res = req:response()
if res.ok then
    print(res.status)           -- integer HTTP status
    local data = res.json       -- decoded JSON value, or nil
    for name, values in pairs(res.headers) do
        print(name, values[1])  -- header names are lowercase; values are arrays
    end
else
    print(res.status, res.error, res.error_code)
    print(res.body)             -- raw response text, if any
end</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>ok</code></td><td>Boolean, <code>true</code> when transport succeeded and the status is 200–299.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>Integer HTTP status code, or zero on transport failure.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>body</code></td><td>Raw response body as a string.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>error</code></td><td>Transport error message, empty for completed HTTP responses including 4xx/5xx.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>error_code</code></td><td>Stable error code string for scripts to branch on.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json</code></td><td>Decoded JSON value when the body parsed; JSON null uses <code>Data.null</code>. Empty or invalid JSON returns <code>nil</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>json_error</code></td><td>JSON parse or conversion error; empty for an empty or valid JSON body. It does not change <code>ok</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>headers</code></td><td>Table of response headers: names lowercased, values arrays of strings.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>HTTPS, redirects, and Android</h2>
<p>In a <code>Task.fork</code> worker, <code>req:wait(timeout_ms)</code> waits on a native completion notification and returns the same response fields as <code>response()</code>, followed by a nil error. Omit the timeout to wait indefinitely; zero checks without blocking. Explicit timeouts must be integers from 0 through 2,147,483,647 milliseconds. A wait timeout returns <code>nil, "timeout"</code>; worker cancellation returns <code>nil, "cancelled"</code>. Neither cancels the request. Transport failures still return a response table: inspect <code>ok</code>, <code>error</code> and <code>error_code</code>. Main VM calls, including its coroutines and completed requests, return <code>nil, error</code>. Create and retain the handle in the worker; HTTP userdata cannot be transferred between VMs.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>HTTPS verifies the certificate chain and hostname.</strong> A mismatch fails the request. Omit <code>ca_certificate_pem</code> to use platform trust, or supply the full bundle you intend to trust for this request. A supplied bundle replaces the platform trust sources.</li>
  <li><strong>3xx redirects are not followed.</strong> The response arrives with its status and <code>Location</code> header so your code can decide to retry — the engine will not silently change the destination of a request.</li>
  <li><strong>Android needs the INTERNET permission.</strong> For a project with explicit Android build settings, declare <code>android.permission.INTERNET</code>; validation reports <code>PROJECT_BUILD_PERMISSION_NETWORK_MISSING</code> when a network service is used without it. See <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'android-builds'}) }}">Android builds</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>TLS client and server</h2>
<p><strong>TLS</strong> (Transport Layer Security) encrypts a byte stream and lets the client verify the server certificate and hostname. Use the TLS services for a custom byte-stream protocol.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>demi.network.tls.client</code> — <code>connect(host, port, trusted_certificate_path[, server_name])</code> starts an outbound TLS connection. Poll <code>events()</code> or <code>is_connected()</code>; <code>send(message)</code> returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>demi.network.tls.server</code> — <code>listen(port, certificate_path, private_key_path[, max_clients])</code> accepts inbound TLS connections. Poll <code>events()</code> for client IDs and use <code>send(client_id, message)</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The TLS bindings resolve certificate arguments as project paths to certificate and key files. This differs from HTTP's <code>ca_certificate_pem</code>, which takes PEM text. The client verifies the supplied trust chain and server name. The server presents its certificate; these APIs do not require client certificates.</p>

<h2>Crypto helpers</h2>
<p>Three small helpers cover the common secrets work of a networked game:</p>
<pre><code>local crypto = require("demi.network.crypto")

local token = crypto.random_token(32)             -- 64 hex characters from 32 random bytes
local salt  = crypto.random_token(16)
local hash  = crypto.password_hash("hunter2", salt) -- PBKDF2-SHA256 hash hex
local same  = crypto.secure_equals(hash, hash)    -- boolean</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>crypto.random_token(n)</code> — returns a lowercase hex string generated from <code>n</code> random bytes (clamped to 1–64); an empty string indicates failure. Use for session tokens and salts.</li>
  <li><code>crypto.password_hash(password, salt[, iterations])</code> — derives a PBKDF2-SHA256 hash and returns its hex string, or an empty string on failure. Store the salt and iteration count alongside the hash; the function does not embed them in its result.</li>
  <li><code>crypto.secure_equals(a, b)</code> — returns a boolean comparison that does not short-circuit on the first differing byte, so timing does not leak how much of a secret matched.</li>
</ul>
<p>Signing material, certificates, and credentials must never enter source, command output, or packaged content.</p>

<h2>Low-level transport</h2>
<p>Under the session layer sits a small transport API in <code>demi.network</code> for building custom protocols:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>host(port[, max_peers])</code> — opens an ENet UDP host and returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>host_dtls(port, certificate_path, private_key_path[, max_peers])</code> — opens an ENet host with DTLS and returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>connect(host, port)</code> or <code>connect_dtls(host, port, trusted_certificate_path[, server_name])</code> — starts a client connection and returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>send(message[, reliable, peer_id, channel])</code> — queues a byte string and returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>events()</code> — drains the transport event queue and returns an array of connect, data, and disconnect events.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most games never need this layer: <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'sessions-and-contracts'}) }}">Sessions &amp; contracts</a> builds replication, validation, and ownership on top of it. Choose the low-level API only when the session model does not fit your protocol.</p>
